# Frozen-Kernel Dynamics: An Interactive Journey

A neural network in the lazy regime can be studied through its residual vector $r_n=f_n-y$. Once the empirical kernel $K$ is fixed, one update is $r_{n+1}=(I-\eta K)r_n$. Start with the simulator, then move from geometric decay to spectral detail and architecture universality.

{{demo:0}}

## 1. See contraction happen

The Geometric Residual Bound says $\|r_n\|\le q^n\|r_0\|$ whenever every update reduces norm by at most a factor $q<1$. Move the slider and notice the qualitative change as $q$ approaches one.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the proof of geometric decay</summary>

At step zero the bound is equality. If $\|r_n\|\le q^n\|r_0\|$, then $\|r_{n+1}\|\le q\|r_n\|\le q^{n+1}\|r_0\|$. Induction proves the estimate, and $q^n\to0$ gives convergence.

</details>

{{algorithm:0}}

## 2. Open the spectral control panel

For an eigenvector with $Kv=\lambda v$, the residual after $n$ steps is exactly $(1-\eta\lambda)^n v$. Explore how the learning rate changes every mode.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the stability classification</summary>

A mode decays exactly when $|1-\eta\lambda|<1$. A negative factor causes alternating signs; a zero eigenvalue gives factor one and an immovable residual component. For a symmetric kernel the global Euclidean contraction factor is $\max_i|1-\eta\lambda_i|$.

</details>

{{algorithm:1}}

For broader background, see the [kernel method overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_method) and the [spectral theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spectral_theorem).

## 3. Watch architecture disappear

Equal frozen kernels, a common target, a common learning rate, and a common initialization force identical prediction paths. More subtly, global equality is unnecessary: the kernel actions need only agree along the realized paths.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Click to reveal the pathwise induction</summary>

Assume the residuals agree at step $n$ and $K_1r_n^{(1)}=K_2r_n^{(2)}$. Subtracting the same scaled correction from the same current residual proves equality at step $n+1$. The common initial residual starts the induction.

</details>

{{algorithm:2}}

## 4. Geometry supplies the frozen interval

If parameter space is divided into tropical cells and the kernel is constant on each cell, then any trajectory staying in its initial cell has $K(\theta(t))=K(\theta(0))$. This geometric certificate activates all the frozen-dynamics results above. Learn more about [tropical geometry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical_geometry).

<details><summary>Click to reveal assumptions and boundaries of the conclusion</summary>

The convergence theorem concerns predictions on a finite training set. Positive semidefiniteness alone is insufficient when the kernel has a nullspace. The results do not claim parameter convergence or off-dataset generalization, and cell invariance freezes the kernel without by itself guaranteeing contraction.

</details>
